.TH FLOWTEST 1
.SH NAME
flowtest \- check flow(2) against the flows that have closed forms
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B flowtest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Flowtest
checks
.IR flow (2)
and prints
.B OK
if everything agrees.
.B -v
prints each measured number.
.PP
Most of fluid mechanics has no closed form, which is why the subject is
computational at all.
Four things here do.
.PP
.B Taylor-Green
is an exact unsteady solution of the
.I full
nonlinear equations: a lattice of vortices decaying as
exp(\-2\fInu t\fP).
It is run on a 16 by 16 grid and a 32 by 32 one, and what is checked is
not a tolerance but the
.IR rate :
halving the spacing must divide the error by about four, which is what
says the scheme is second order in space rather than merely convergent.
It divides it by 3.875.
The energy is separately checked to decay as exp(\-4\fInu t\fP), twice
the rate of the velocity.
.PP
.B Poiseuille
is a channel under a steady push, which settles into the parabola
.IR gy ( H \- y )/2\fInu\fP.
.PP
.B Couette
is the same channel with a sliding lid instead, which gives a straight
line \- and gives it
.I exactly,
since a unidirectional flow's nonlinear term vanishes identically.
It comes out right to 1.5e\-14, which is the strongest single number
here.
.PP
.B Mass
is not an approximation to be checked against a tolerance.
After the projection, zero divergence is an identity, and it sits at
about 1e\-15.
If it drifts, mass is being made.
.PP
The lid-driven cavity has no closed form at all, which is why it is the
standard benchmark.
What can be checked without one is checked: mass is conserved, the flow
settles, nothing passes through a wall, the fluid under the lid goes the
way the lid pushes it and the flow along the floor comes back the other
way.
.SS The test that had to be invented
Nothing in the list above exercises the advection term, and deleting it
outright left every one of them passing.
.PP
That is not an oversight in the choice of cases so much as a property of
them.
Taylor-Green's own nonlinear term is a pure gradient, which the pressure
projection absorbs completely; Poiseuille and Couette are
unidirectional, so theirs vanishes identically.
The three standard flows that have closed forms are exactly the three
where advection cannot be seen.
.PP
What sees it is the same vortex with a uniform drift added.
Galilean invariance makes that an exact solution too \- the pattern is
carried along while it decays \- and the carrying is done by the
advection term and by nothing else.
At the settings used the pattern moves one radian in a box of 2 pi, and
with the advection deleted it stays where it was: the error goes from
0.007 to 0.47.
.SS And one about where the samples are taken
Every sample of the channel profile sat exactly at a cell centre, where
the interpolation weights come out whole and it does not matter which
pair of cells was chosen.
Sampled at twenty-one generic heights it does matter: taking the nearest
cell rather than the one below turns an interpolation into an
extrapolation, and the worst error goes from 9.6e\-4 to 3.8e\-3.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/flowtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR flow (2),
.IR krylov (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each disagreement prints a line beginning
.B FAIL
naming what was measured, what came out and what was wanted; the count
follows and the program raises an exception.
.SH BUGS
Every flow here is smooth and slow.
Nothing is run at a Reynolds number high enough for the centred
advection to produce the cell-by-cell oscillation that
.IR flow (2)'s
BUGS describes, so that failure mode is described and not demonstrated.
.PP
The order in time is not measured.
Only the spatial order is, by refining the grid at a fixed step; halving
the step at a fixed grid would measure the other, and does not happen.
.PP
The cavity is checked for settling, for conserving mass and for
circulating the right way, but not against the published profiles for
it.
A solver that was wrong in a way that preserved all three would pass.
